In-place Buddy Allocator

A buddy allocator that needs no external bookkeeping memory: the free lists live inside the free blocks themselves, so the memory buffer is the only state.

Allocator for embedded or other memory constrained systems. Both lookups (by size and by address) are kept in tree structures stored in the free blocks.

Usage:

    const buddy = @import("buddy.zig");

    // 1024 blocks of 64 bytes (on 64-bit targets), block 0 is reserved for bookkeeping
    const heap = comptime buddy.BuddyAllocator(1024);

    // once, before first use
    heap.reset();

    // error.OutOfMemory when nothing fits
    const buf = try heap.malloc(100);

    // pass the same slice back, not just the pointer
    heap.free(buf);

IMPORTANT
0. Call reset() once before first use, memory starts uninitialized. Block count must be a power of two.
1. No safety checks are available, (ex. freeing memory that does not belong to allocator does not raise any errors).
2. Tracking allocated memory size is a burden of a caller, i.e. you must free the slice you have allocated, not just a pointer
3. Probably contains bugs even though I wrote tests that cover basic use-cases and I used it in my small project which never left the debugging stage, so no promises - use at you own risk!

run tests 
zig test buddy.zig 
